### DRF scheduler request samples (REST Client / IntelliJ .http style)
### Base: sim-mode server started with:
###   go run ./cmd/drfscheduler -capacity-cpu 9000 -capacity-mem 18000

### Health
GET http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz

### Set / change cluster capacity (rejected with 422 if below current usage)
PUT http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/cluster/capacity
Content-Type: application/json

{"cpu_millicpu": 9000, "memory_mib": 18000}

### Create tenants with explicit weights
PUT http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/tenants/A
Content-Type: application/json

{"weight": 1}

PUT http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/tenants/B
Content-Type: application/json

{"weight": 2}

### List tenants (includes exact dominant / weighted shares)
GET http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/tenants

### Submit tasks (sim mode uses duration_ms; id optional)
POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{"id": "A1", "tenant_id": "A", "cpu_millicpu": 1000, "memory_mib": 4000, "duration_ms": 100}

### Batch submit: all tasks arrive at the same virtual instant.
### The first scheduling pass sees the whole batch (atomic; one bad
### element rejects the entire request with no partial admission).
POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/tasks/batch
Content-Type: application/json

[
  {"id": "A1", "tenant_id": "A", "cpu_millicpu": 1000, "memory_mib": 4000, "duration_ms": 100},
  {"id": "B1", "tenant_id": "B", "cpu_millicpu": 3000, "memory_mib": 1000, "duration_ms": 200}
]

### A task larger than total capacity -> 422 (never queued)POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{"tenant_id": "A", "cpu_millicpu": 999999, "memory_mib": 1, "duration_ms": 100}

### Unknown tenant -> 404
POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{"tenant_id": "ghost", "cpu_millicpu": 1, "memory_mib": 1, "duration_ms": 100}

### Full state snapshot with the no-overcommit invariant
GET http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/state

### Filter tasks
GET http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/tasks?tenant_id=A&state=QUEUED

### Single task
GET http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/tasks/A1

### Cancel a queued task
POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/tasks/A1/cancel
Content-Type: application/json

{"reason": "user cancelled"}

### Structured state-change events
GET http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/events

### Manually trigger a scheduling pass
POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/schedule

### Advance the fake clock (sim mode only; 400 in real mode)
POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/clock/advance
Content-Type: application/json

{"advance_ms": 100}
